            <ArticleTitle>Huge epidural hematoma after surgery for spinal cord stimulation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>565-7; discussion 567</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE AND IMPORTANCE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Spinal epidural haematoma (SEH) following implantation of an epidural spinal cord electrode is a very rare complication but one that must not be overlooked. This case is unusual because of the almost &quot;holocord&quot; extension of the haematoma and the excellent recovery obtained by prompt surgical treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL PRESENTATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 69 years old man with normal serum coagulation parameters was submitted to spinal cord stimulation (SCS) for chronic pain syndrome. After a minimal L1 laminotomy the patient developed paraplegia due to a large haematoma at D4-L2.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Surgical removal of the entire clot by a D4-L2 laminectomy was performed immediately.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Large epidural haematoma can result from SCS and this complication may be cured by appropriate and prompt surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
